Immediately he called them. Let their father Zebedee sit in the boat with the hired servants. And they followed Jesus. Jesus casts out a demon.
Christ and those with him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he went to the synagogue. There he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
At that moment, a man in their temple screamed. An evil spirit had possessed him. He said,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are: the Holy One of God!
"Be quiet!" said Jesus firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a scream.
Everyone was astonished, and they asked one another, "What is this? A new teaching, with authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey him."
The news about Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus is healing many people.
Christ and his disciples went out of the synagogue. They went along with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her. He took her hand and helped her up. The fever had left her. Afterwards, she began to serve them.
In the evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. They also brought those who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered in front of the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They had all kinds of diseases. He even cast out many demons. But he wouldn't let them speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
Bakri was very early in the morning and it was still dark. Issa got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
They found him, they called out to him, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let's go somewhere else. I want to go to the nearby towns. I must preach there also. That is why I came."
Jesus heals a man who had a disease in his skin.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt before Jesus and begged him, saying, "If you are willing to heal me and make my skin clean, you can do it."
Jesus was deeply concerned. He reached out his hand and touched the man. He said to him, "I am willing to do it." "Become pure!"
Illness immediately struck him. He sought treatment.
Jesus told him to leave now. He gave him a strong warning.
"Don't tell anyone this," he said. "Don't show your head to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. It will be a testimony to the priest and to the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to spread the news now. He spread the word to everyone. And Jesus could no longer enter any city publicly. He remained outside in isolated places. But people still kept coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After some days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned home.
A large crowd gathered until there was no more room. There was not even any more room outside the door. And Jesus was preaching the word to them.
A quarter of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get the man close enough to Jesus because of the crowd. So they cut a hole in the roof right above Jesus. Then they lowered the man down through the hole on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
There were some teachers of the law sitting there. They were reasoning within themselves,
Why is this man speaking in this way? He's telling me something very bad! Only God can forgive sins!
Immediately Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why are you thinking these things?"
Which is easier, to tell this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Get up, take your mat and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then, Jesus called the man who could not walk.
"I'm telling you," he said, "get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he went out, and everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus called Levi.
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." Levi got up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Levi's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. They asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard about this. He said to them, "Those who are in good health do not need a physician; but those who are sick do need one. I did not come to call the righteous to repentance, but I came to call the sinners to repentance." They asked Jesus about fasting.
The disciples of John and of the Pharisees were fasting; and they came and said to Him, "The disciples of John fast often and make prayers; likewise also the disciples of the Pharisees fast often and make prayers. But Your disciples fast not; and they pray not. Why is it that Your disciples do neither fast nor make prayers?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will take them away. On that day, they will leave without eating.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. If they did, the new piece would be dragged out by the old. This would increase the tearing.
People don't put new wine into old wine skins. If they did, the wine would burst the skins. Then the wine and the skins would both be ruined. No, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On the Sabbath, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the grainfields. The disciples began to pick some of the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, it is not lawful to do what you are doing on the Sabbath. Why are your disciples doing it?"
Answer, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food.
The time when Ahimaz was the priest. David went into the house of God and ate the most holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David even gave some to his men.
And Jesus said to them afterward, "The Sabbath was made for humankind. Humankind was not made for the Sabbath.
So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.
Healing on the Sabbath.
Again Jesus went to the synagogue. There was a man there who had a withered hand.
Some Pharisees were looking for a fault in Jesus. They were watching him carefully. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus spoke to the man with the weak, crooked hand. He said to him, "Stand in front of all the people."
Afterward, they asked them, "What does the Law say we must do on the Sabbath? Is it right to do good or evil? Is it right to save life or destroy it?" But they remained silent.
Jesus looked at them in anger. He was very upset because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored like the other.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus. The crowds were following Jesus.
Jesus walked to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A large crowd from Galilee followed him.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. And many people came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and Idumea. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to find him a small boat. This would prevent people from crowding him.
Jesus healed many people. That is why those who were sick were crowding to touch him.
Many people who had evil spirits in them saw him; they fell down before him. The spirits shrieked, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus ordered them not to say who he was. Jesus appointed the twelve apostles.
He went up a mountain. He called certain people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed 12 of them and named them apostles. From then on, they were with him. And he was going to send them out to preach.
They will have the authority to cast out demons.
Now Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
James and John, the sons of Zebedee, and their brother James. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
They were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And they were also Judas the Zealot and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He is the one who will betray Jesus to his enemies afterwards. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Once again a crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this. They went to take care of him. They said, "He is sick in the head."
There were some teachers of the law there. They had come down from Jerusalem. They said, "He is controlled by Beelzebul! He drives out demons by the power of the prince of demons."
Jesus came, called out to them, and spoke to them in parables. He said to them, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If the Kingdom is fighting against itself, it will no longer stand.
If the family is scattered, it cannot endure.
